<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tarikh-i Sistan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as History of Sistan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tarikh-i <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a> is an anonymous Persian chronicle of the province of Sistan in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, written in stages from the 11th to the 14th century. It traces the region's history from legendary times, including the hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rustam</a>, through the Arab conquest and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarid dynasty</a>. It is a major source for the Saffarids and an important early work of Persian prose.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tarikh-i-sistan.jpg" alt="Tarikh-i Sistan" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: محمد تقی بهار · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarikh-e-Sistan_Bahar.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
